T:What it is
T:A password,
T:sometimes
T:called a
T:passcode, is
T:secret data,
T:typically a
T:string of
T:characters,
T:usually used
T:to confirm a
T:user's
T:identity.
T:Traditional,
T:passwords
T:were
T:expected to
T:be
T:memorized,
T:but the
T:large number
T:of
T:password-pro
T:services
T:that a
T:typical
T:individual
T:accesses can
T:make
T:memorization
T:of unique
T:passwords
T:for each
T:service
T:impractical.
T:Using the
T:terminology
T:of the NIST
T:Digital
T:Identity
T:Guidelines,
T:the secret
T:is held by a
T:party called
T:the claimant
T:while the
T:party
T:verifying
T:the identity
T:of the
T:claimant is
T:called the
T:verifier.
T:When the
T:claimant
T:successfully
T:demonstrates
T:knowledge of
T:the password
T:to the
T:verifier
T:through an
T:established
T:authenticati
T:protocol,
T:the verifier
T:can infer
T:the
T:claimant's
T:identity.
